# json.oro — a JSON encoder/decoder.
#
# API
# ---
#   json.parse(text)                   -> dict | list | str | int | float | bool | null
#   json.stringify(value, indent=null) -> str
#
# `stringify` takes one optional `indent` rather than shipping a second
# `stringify_pretty` (or `dumps`/`dumps_pretty`) function: pretty-printing is
# a formatting *detail* of the single operation "turn a value into JSON
# text", not a second operation, the same way `sorted(key=, reverse=)` stays
# one function instead of growing a `sorted_by`. `indent=null` (the default)
# is the compact form — no incidental whitespace at all, not even the
# `", "`/`": "` CPython's `json.dumps()` inserts by default — because
# "compact" was asked for directly and a genuinely minimal wire format is a
# real, distinct use case from CPython's default. Passing an int number of
# spaces switches to one-value-per-line pretty output. That covers both real
# needs (wire format, readable output) with one name.
#
# Numbers: any literal containing `.`, `e`, or `E` decodes to a float;
# everything else decodes to an int (arbitrary precision, exactly like the
# rest of Oro — an integer too large for a machine word promotes to bignum).
# Malformed input raises ValueError naming the character offset, e.g.
# `unexpected '}' at position 14`.
#
# Dict keys: `stringify` requires str keys and raises TypeError otherwise,
# rather than silently stringifying an int/bool/float key the way CPython's
# `json.dumps` does — guessing a conversion is exactly the kind of second
# spelling the language avoids elsewhere (see the `to_` casts).
#
# Depth: `parse` accepts 10 000 levels of nesting and raises ValueError past
# that. A document arriving off a socket chooses its own shape, and one byte
# of `[` buys a heap container; the cap bounds that amplification. Nothing
# real nests past about thirty.
#
# --- Why the loop below is in Rust ----------------------------------------
#
# This module used to be a character-at-a-time parser written in Oro, and it
# was the standard library's flagship example of "stdlib written in Oro on
# top of the frozen core". It measured 95x CPython's C `json` on a 1 KB
# payload, which turned `json` into more of a realistic API handler's CPU
# than the HTTP layer, the router and the interpreter put together.
#
# `docs/stdlib-server-design.md` §5's rule — *anything that touches every
# byte goes in Rust; anything that touches every request goes in Oro* — was
# never wrong. It simply was not applied here: a JSON parser is a per-byte
# loop, and per-byte loops were always the Rust half. §5 now says so, and
# says why this does not reopen the case for a Rust `http` module: an HTTP
# head is not a per-byte loop at the Oro level (it decomposes into three
# generic `bytes` calls), and it carries policy a server author must be able
# to read and change. A JSON document has exactly one reading, so there is no
# policy here to take away from anyone.
#
# What stayed in Oro is what this file still is: the module's surface, its
# documentation, its defaults and its argument shapes. `_json` is the loop,
# and it is not language surface — an underscored built-in resolves only from
# inside a stdlib module body, so user code cannot reach it.
import _json


# Decode JSON text.
def parse(text):
    return _json.parse(text)


# Encode a value as JSON text; `indent` spaces per level, or null for the
# compact form.
def stringify(value, indent=null):
    return _json.stringify(value, indent)
